2026-05-24 02:16:12,833 - INFO - thoughts: ['```python\n# 1. Debt-to-Income Ratio\n# Feature description: Create a new feature that calculates the ratio of Debt to Income (Debt / Income), \n# providing insight into financial stability. This value will help determine if individuals have a manageable \n# level of financial obligations compared to their earnings. We\'ll handle cases where Income is zero by \n# assigning a large value to indicate financial risk or replace with an indicative constant.\n# Usefulness: Debt-to-Income ratio is a crucial metric analyzed by financial institutions to assess risk. \n# High debt proportion relative to income signals financial instability, which may correlate with success \n# in credit card applications.\n\ndf[\'DebtIncomeRatio\'] = df[\'Debt\'] / (df[\'Income\'] + 1e-6)  # Adding a small constant to prevent division by zero.\n```end\n\n```python\n# 2. Employment Stability\n# Feature description: Compute a new feature based on whether an individual is employed and the duration of employment.\n# If Employed is 1 (True), multiply it by YearsEmployed to reflect employment stability. If not employed (Employed=0), \n# assign a 0. This feature emphasizes the impact of not just employment status but also employment history.\n# Usefulness: Stability in employment often influences success in credit applications. Longer employment history \n# indicates reliability and earning consistency, positively affecting creditworthiness.\n\ndf[\'EmploymentStability\'] = df[\'Employed\'] * df[\'YearsEmployed\']\n```end\n\n```python\n# 3. Income Categorization - Income Binning\n# Feature description: Categorize Income into bins using \'bin\' operation to capture ranges like:\n# "Low," "Medium," "High," etc. This transforms continuous Income into discrete categories useful for non-linear \n# relationships with credit card approval rates. For instance, higher Income categories may signify greater ability \n# to pay credit bills timely or access additional credit.\n# Usefulness: Income binning groups applicants based on their financial capabilities in clearer granularity \n# (e.g., distinguishing higher earners vs low-income applicants). This improves downstream model interpretability \n# and potentially enhances predictive power.\n\ndf[\'IncomeBin\'] = pd.cut(df[\'Income\'], bins=[-1, 100, 500, 2000, np.inf], labels=[\'Low\', \'Medium\', \'High\', \'Very High\'])\n```end\n\n### Dropping unhelpful column(s)\n```python\n# Explanation why the column ZipCode is dropped\n# The feature ZipCode is not directly relevant to predicting credit card application success in the dataset.\n# ZIP codes are indirectly correlated to factors like regional income but aren\'t useful on their own in this \n# context due to lack of contextual differentiation. This column adds noise instead of actionable insights.\n\ndf.drop(columns=[\'ZipCode\'], inplace=True)\n```end', "```python\n# Step 1: Interaction Feature - Debt-to-Income Ratio\n# Feature name: DebtToIncomeRatio\n# Feature description: This new feature is calculated as the ratio of Debt to Income (Debt / Income). It captures the relationship between a person's outstanding debt and their income. Individuals with higher Debt-to-Income Ratios may have lower chances of credit card approval, as it might indicate financial strain.\n# Usefulness: Adding this feature provides insight into the applicant's debt burden in relation to their income. It is a common metric used by financial institutions to assess creditworthiness. This will likely help the model better differentiate between successful and unsuccessful applicants.\n\ndf['DebtToIncomeRatio'] = df['Debt'] / (df['Income'] + 0.01)  # Adding small constant to avoid division by 0.\n```end\n\n```python\n# Step 2: YearsEmployed Recency Transformation\n# Feature name: YearsEmployedLog\n# Feature description: Calculates the log transformation of YearsEmployed. Using logarithms reduces the impact of outliers for individuals with long employment histories, while emphasizing smaller differences for those with shorter tenure.\n# Usefulness: Employment stability is a key factor in credit card approval. Applying log transformation makes this metric more robust and reduces skewness caused by extreme values, thus improving its predictive power in the model.\n\ndf['YearsEmployedLog'] = np.log(df['YearsEmployed'] + 1)  # Adding 1 to avoid log(0) issues.\n```end\n\n```python\n# Step 3: Industry-Based Aggregation Feature\n# Feature name: IndustryCreditScoreMean\n# Feature description: This feature calculates the mean CreditScore for applicants within the same Industry group.\n# Usefulness: Different industries may have varying economic stability and distinct profiles of creditworthiness. Adding this aggregated feature based on Industry provides the model with context regarding the average credit behavior or stability within that sector, helping the model better predict successful applicants' likelihood.\n\ndf['IndustryCreditScoreMean'] = df.groupby('Industry')['CreditScore'].transform('mean')\n```end\n\n```python\n# Dropping Column: DriversLicense\n# Explanation why the column DriversLicense is dropped\ndf.drop(columns=['DriversLicense'], inplace=True)\n# Reason: DriversLicense likely has limited correlation with credit card application success. Its presence may act as noise, and removing it reduces irrelevant features in the dataset, allowing the model to focus on more relevant predictors.\n```end\n\n```python\n# Dropping Column: ZipCode\n# Explanation why the column ZipCode is dropped\ndf.drop(columns=['ZipCode'], inplace=True)\n# Reason: ZipCode as a raw numerical feature has little predictive value, as its meaning is location-based and categorical. Keeping it without transformation does not positively contribute to a predictive model. Geographical-based borrowing patterns might instead be represented by aggregated features or one-hot encoding, not raw ZipCode.\n```end\n\n```python\n# Dropping Column: Ethnicity\n# Explanation why the column Ethnicity is dropped\ndf.drop(columns=['Ethnicity'], inplace=True)\n# Reason: Ethnicity is sensitive demographic information that must be excluded to prevent introducing bias or discrimination in the model. Its removal ensures ethical compliance while allowing other features to drive predictive insights.\n```end\n\nThese feature engineering steps aim to improve the predictive performance of the model, simplify the dataset, and introduce more meaningful attributes while handling ethical concerns appropriately."]
