 geographical code, but it is not informative without additional geographical context (e.g., mapping to income level, urban density, or other regional statistics). In its current form, it is unlikely to provide meaningful signals for predicting credit card application success.\ndf.drop(columns=['ZipCode'], inplace=True)\n```end\n\n--- \n\nThese steps focus on adding high-value derived features while decluttering irrelevant information to improve the predictive power of your model.", '```python\n# Step 1: Generate a "Debt-to-Income Ratio" feature\ndf[\'Debt_Income_Ratio\'] = df[\'Debt\'] / (df[\'Income\'] + 1e-5)  # To avoid division by zero\n# Usefulness: A "Debt-to-Income Ratio" feature adds essential context about a person\'s financial stability.\n# Applicants who have a high debt compared to their income might be less likely to be approved for a credit card.\n# This feature provides a normalized context rather than only raw Debt or Income as standalone features.\n```end\n\n```python\n# Step 2: Generate a "Employment-to-Age Ratio" feature\ndf[\'Employment_Age_Ratio\'] = df[\'YearsEmployed\'] / (df[\'Age\'] + 1e-5)  # To avoid division by zero\n# Usefulness: This feature indicates the proportion of a person’s lifetime that they have been employed.\n# A higher ratio suggests a stable employment history relative to age, which might correlate with creditworthiness or income.\n# Useful especially for younger applicants to compare across different age groups in a normalized way.\n```end\n\n```python\n# Step 3: Perform One-Hot Encoding for "Industry"\nindustry_dummies = pd.get_dummies(df[\'Industry\'], prefix=\'Industry\')\ndf = pd.concat([df, industry_dummies], axis=1)\n# Usefulness: The "Industry" feature provides subtle distinctions between different sectors of employment.\n# Some industries might correlate positively with financial stability or income level, which can affect creditworthiness.\n# Creating one-hot encoded features allows the model to weight each industry independently instead of treating it as a single categorical variable.\n```end\n\n```python\n# Explanation why the column "ZipCode" is dropped\ndf.drop(columns=[\'ZipCode\'], inplace=True)\n# ZipCode is dropped because it adds little to no predictive value about the applicant\'s creditworthiness.\n# While geographical information can sometimes be useful, in this case, the dataset does not provide sufficient context\n# (e.g., cost of living or regional economic indicators) for the ZipCode values. Retaining it may just add noise.\n```end\n\n```python\n# Explanation why the column "DriversLicense" is dropped\ndf.drop(columns=[\'DriversLicense\'], inplace=True)\n# DriversLicense is dropped because it is not strongly tied to a person\'s creditworthiness.\n# Licensing status may not be directly related to credit behavior or financial stability. It likely adds noise to the prediction task.\n```end\n\n```python\n# Explanation why "Citizen" is transformed with label encoding\nfrom sklearn.preprocessing import LabelEncoder\nle = LabelEncoder()\ndf[\'Citizen_Encoded\'] = le.fit_transform(df[\'Citizen\'])\n# Usefulness: "Citizen" is a categorical feature with meaningful ordinal relationships (e.g., Temporary vs. ByBirth).\n# Encoding it numerically maps these underlying relationships for the model while preserving the hierarchy.\n# This is simpler than one-hot encoding and more appropriate for this particular feature.\n```end']
